{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3085 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0899d9ca4e1cfd6abe62b5c61aad43ecd3613866e57725c07846b527f361e298" :edb/db-before-t 3084 :edb/db-after-t 3085 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536397 50 "2026-10-17T00:52:30.029Z" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059696 1038 17592186571388 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059696 1038 17592186571451 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059697 1038 17592186571400 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059697 1038 17592186571402 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059697 1038 17592186571418 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059698 1038 17592186571384 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059698 1038 17592186571428 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059698 1038 17592186571439 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186059911 1050 420.0 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186060982 1064 "**De Morgan's laws** for logic tell us how to distribute a negation over a conjunction or disjunction.\n\n- De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B.\n$$\n\n- De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, you can think that the negation \"flips\" the conjunction into a disjunction, and vice versa.\n\nTo prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables:\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\land B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\lor B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\land \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186060982 1064 "**De Morgan's laws** for logic tell us how to distribute a negation over a conjunction or disjunction.\n\n- De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that $$\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B.$$\n- De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that $$\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B.$$\n\nIntuitively, you can think that the negation \"flips\" the conjunction into a disjunction, and vice versa.\n\nTo prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables:\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\land B) \\equiv \\neg A \\lor \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\land B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\n- The following truth table shows that $\\neg ( A \\lor B) \\equiv \\neg A \\land \\neg B{:}$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg ( A \\lor B) \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\land \\neg B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |" 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186064323 1038 17592186571406 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186064324 1038 17592186571411 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186064324 1038 17592186571445 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068756 1038 17592186571383 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068757 1038 17592186571444 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068758 1038 17592186571435 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 17592186571395 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068815 1038 17592186571419 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 17592186571377 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 17592186571417 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186068816 1038 17592186571430 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186073784 1038 17592186571381 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186073917 1038 17592186571379 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186074207 1070 17592186045487 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186074219 1070 17592186045485 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078436 1038 17592186571427 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078436 1038 17592186571452 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078498 1038 17592186571415 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078498 1038 17592186571429 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078499 1038 17592186571409 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078499 1038 17592186571449 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078523 1038 17592186571422 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078523 1038 17592186571453 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078565 1038 17592186571403 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186078696 1050 420.0 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186079673 1064 "Suppose we want to find the surface area of a cube whose edges are $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in length, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e727b20f63c46ef6124d2d33f5db0a003d798f69ec988ee6b41b651e34456387.png)\n\nThe surface area of the cube is the area of all the faces combined. In general, for a cube with sides of length $s$, there are $6$ sides, each with area\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = s^2\n$$\n\nSo, the surface area is given by\n\n$$\nS = 6\\mathcal A =6 s^2\n$$\n\nTo find the surface area of the cube above, we substitute\n\n$$\ns=3 \\: \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula. This gives\n\n$$\nS = 6(3\\, \\text{cm})^2 = 54 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the cube is $54\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$." 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186079673 1064 "Suppose we want to find the surface area of a cube whose edges are $3\\,\\textrm{cm}$ in length, like the one shown below. \n\n![](images/e7/e727b20f63c46ef6124d2d33f5db0a003d798f69ec988ee6b41b651e34456387.png)\n\nThe surface area of the cube is the area of all the faces combined. In general, for a cube with sides of length $s,$ there are $6$ sides, each with area $\\mathcal A = s^2.$ So, the surface area is given by\n\n$$\nS = 6\\mathcal A =6 s^2.\n$$\n\nTo find the surface area of the cube above, we substitute $s=3 \\: \\textrm{cm}$ into the formula. This gives\n\n$$\nS = 6(3\\, \\text{cm})^2 = 54 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the cube is $54\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.$" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186079681 1064 "The **lateral surface area** of a cube is the surface area of the side faces alone. In other words, it's the surface area that does not include the area of the top or bottom faces.\n\n![](images/78/78fbc4013c4f625f7e5b839ed7545cc6c8a476d31a7f0f5bda8645d73785f70e.png)\n\nThe lateral surface area $S_L$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = 4s^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side." 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186079681 1064 "The **lateral surface area** of a cube is the surface area of the side faces alone. In other words, it's the surface area that does not include the area of the top or bottom faces.\n\n![](images/78/78fbc4013c4f625f7e5b839ed7545cc6c8a476d31a7f0f5bda8645d73785f70e.png)\n\nThe lateral surface area $S_L$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nS_L = 4s^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the length of a side." 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 17592186571382 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186083515 1070 17592186045485 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096617 1038 17592186571442 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096618 1038 17592186571385 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096618 1038 17592186571450 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096619 1038 17592186571432 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096643 1038 17592186571392 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096643 1038 17592186571426 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096643 1038 17592186571436 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096643 1038 17592186571437 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096881 1050 960.0 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186096889 1050 840.0 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186097805 1064 "![](images/0a/0ac79071675ca17dbb25708f8131a0f1706c29ffe69c750969112fd6a097d1f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{b} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow can we find $\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} \\mid$?\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/dd/dd9b407a04394d9648db4671788791a8cf615b7755c1efead6f637fe26a40562.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. But we can use the law of cosines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos (120^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 4^{2} + 5^{2} - 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= 16 + 25 + 20 \\\\\n&= 61\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{61}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186097805 1064 "![](images/0a/0ac79071675ca17dbb25708f8131a0f1706c29ffe69c750969112fd6a097d1f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $|\\mathbf{a}|=4$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=5.$ How can we find $|\\mathbf{a+b}|?$ \n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/dd/dd9b407a04394d9648db4671788791a8cf615b7755c1efead6f637fe26a40562.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. But we can use the law of cosines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =16+25+20 \\\\  & =61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|\\mathbf{a+b}|=\\sqrt{61}.$" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186097818 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{'}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186097818 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186097829 1064 "Suppose we have a vector\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\textbf{a}\n$$\n\nWe denote its **magnitude**, also known as its *modulus* or *length*, as $\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} \\mid$ or $\\mid \\textbf{a} \\mid$.\n\nFor example, the vector below has a magnitude of $8$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/71105c9d5920ec71e61fe1176021024188a86bd8023671b79e0ce89082994299.png)\n\nNow, let's consider the following situation. Suppose that the vector $\\textbf{a}$ is directed vertically upwards and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{a} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile $\\textbf{b}$ is directed horizontally to the right and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{b} &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow can we find $\\mid \\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b} \\mid$?\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find the resultant $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/ff/ffd155d3b72015a72d878fc8089381a5bcf5a97f698f85945642189ea7368a3f.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 6^{2} + 8^{2} \\\\\n&= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b} &= \\sqrt{100}=10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: In the above calculation we wrote\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & ^2 = & \\mathbf{a} & ^2+\\left.\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{b}\\right|^2\n$$\n\n. This works only when the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are at right angles, and the relationship is not true in general. We'll see how to deal with non-right-angled vectors soon." 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186097829 1064 "Suppose we have a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\textbf{a}.$ We denote its **magnitude**, also known as its *modulus* or *length*, as $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$ or $|\\textbf{a}|.$\n\nFor example, the vector below has a magnitude of $8,$ denoted $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=8.$\n\n![](images/71/71105c9d5920ec71e61fe1176021024188a86bd8023671b79e0ce89082994299.png)\n\nNow, let's consider the following situation. Suppose that the vector $\\textbf{a}$ is directed vertically upwards and $|\\textbf{a}|=6,$ while $\\textbf{b}$ is directed horizontally to the right and $|\\textbf{b}|=8.$ How can we find $|\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}|?$\n\nFirst, we use the triangle law of addition to find the resultant $\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ff/ffd155d3b72015a72d878fc8089381a5bcf5a97f698f85945642189ea7368a3f.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{a}+\\text{b}{|}^{2} & =|\\text{a}{|}^{2}+|\\text{b}{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={6}^{2}+{8}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\textbf{a}+\\textbf{b}|=\\sqrt{100}=10.\n$$\n\n**Important**: In the above calculation we wrote $|\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}|^2 = |\\mathbf{a}|^2+|\\mathbf{b}|^2$. This works only when the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are at right angles, and the relationship is not true in general. We'll see how to deal with non-right-angled vectors soon." 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186097839 1064 "When the triangle law for addition does *not* give a right triangle, we *cannot* use the Pythagorean theorem to compute the magnitude of the sum.\n\nFor example, using the triangle law to draw the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ for the vectors below, we do not get a right triangle. Instead, we get a triangle with an obtuse angle $\\theta$.\n\n![](images/96/967966ec9d8ac0b7358623645de0e7931ef4d6cc87bd919d1f0cf04f7ca6ca28.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. However, we can use the law of cosines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given any two vectors that form a triangle using the triangle addition law, we can always use the law of cosines to compute the magnitude of the sum." 13194139536397 17592186045417 false] [17592186097839 1064 "When the triangle law for addition does *not* give a right triangle, we *cannot* use the Pythagorean theorem to compute the magnitude of the sum.\n\nFor example, using the triangle law to draw the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ for the vectors below, we do not get a right triangle. Instead, we get a triangle with an obtuse angle $\\theta.$\n\n![](images/96/967966ec9d8ac0b7358623645de0e7931ef4d6cc87bd919d1f0cf04f7ca6ca28.png)\n\nWe do not obtain a right triangle here, so we cannot use the Pythagorean theorem. However, we can use the law of cosines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2}-2|a||b|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given any two vectors that form a triangle using the triangle addition law, we can always use the law of cosines to compute the magnitude of the sum." 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186105718 1038 17592186571431 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186105719 1038 17592186571389 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186105769 1038 17592186571393 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186105770 1038 17592186571446 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186106315 1070 17592186045485 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119771 1038 17592186571401 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119772 1038 17592186571387 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119772 1038 17592186571390 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119773 1038 17592186571454 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119774 1038 17592186571396 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119774 1038 17592186571440 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119781 1038 17592186571421 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119782 1038 17592186571408 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186119783 1038 17592186571404 13194139536397 17592186045417 t